WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.900
How do things move in real life?

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:09.360
Say a ball is moving on a flat ground,

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.600
and there is no external force acting on it.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:15.400
Will the ball eventually come to a halt?

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:19.220
Yes, it will. Have you ever wondered why?

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:21.500
Say you're skating on an ice rink

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:23.460
and your friend is skating on the road.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:25.980
Whose movement do you think will be smoother?

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:28.180
Definitely yours!

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:30.440
When you hold a bottle in your hand,

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.960
why doesn't it just slip through and fall?

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.540
Sometimes when you're walking around in a mall,

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:38.460
do you remember seeing a caution sign to warn you

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.360
about the floor being wet?

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:42.360
What explains all this?

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:44.800
The answer is FRICTION!

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:47.720
The reason why the ball eventually comes to a halt

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:50.100
is because of the force of friction.

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:54.020
The reason why skating on an ice rink is easier

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:57.000
because the force of Friction is lesser there.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:59.140
The bottle doesn't slip through

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:02.140
because the force of friction between your hand and the bottle

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:04.540
doesn't allow it to do so.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:08.740
If the floor is wet the friction offered by it reduces

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:11.840
and there are chances that you might slip and fall.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:14.660
So what is this friction that we're talking about?

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.920
It's quite easy to understand its concept.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.060
Friction is nothing but a FORCE.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:25.400
It is the force exerted by a surface where an object moves across it.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:29.020
In the first case, when the ball is moving in this direction,

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:33.200
the force of friction is acting in the opposite direction.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:36.540
The force of friction offered by the ice rink floor

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:39.720
is lesser than the force of friction offered by the road.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:44.540
Hence skating on the ice rink is easier and seems effortless!

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:46.800
The bottle does not slip down

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.200
because the surface of your hand offers a friction in the upward direction.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:53.600
And in the case of the floor in the mall,

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:57.500
the soapy water reduces the friction offered by the floor.

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:02.040
In the coming lessons, we'll learn a lot more about friction.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:04.740
We'll see the factors affecting friction.

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:09.740
We'll understand if friction helps us in our everyday life or not.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:14.060
And we will also cover the different types of friction.

